/// \defgroup grp_w1024a_wwmath_gridcull WWMath: the grid culling system
/// \ingroup grp_w3d_rendering
/// \brief A uniform grid of cells that finds the objects near a point, an
///        axis-aligned box, an oriented box or a frustum, built for objects
///        that move.
///
/// WWMath's culling systems answer one question: which of the objects
/// registered with me touch this volume? GridCullSystemClass (gridcull.cpp,
/// declared in gridcull.h) is the one designed for objects that move. It
/// divides a box-shaped world into equal cells and files each object under the
/// single cell that holds the centre of its cull box, so adding, moving and
/// removing an object all take constant time. Westwood's header comment states
/// the trade-off: the grid must divide space uniformly, so it culls less
/// tightly than its sibling AABTreeCullSystemClass (aabtreecull.cpp), which
/// follows the geometry put into it but needs logarithmic time to insert. The
/// W3D renderer's geometry layer is described in \ref sub_w3d_rendering.
///
/// \par The pieces
/// - CullableClass (cullsys.h) is anything that can be put in a culling
///   system. It carries a cull box, an AABoxClass that its owner guarantees
///   contains the object; a pointer to the link record the owning system
///   keeps for it; and a chain pointer used while it sits in a collection.
///   CullableClass::Set_Cull_Box stores a new box and, unless the caller says
///   the object was just loaded, calls the owning system's Update_Culling, so
///   moving an object means setting its cull box.
/// - GridLinkClass (gridcull.h) is the grid's link record: the object's
///   cell address and the previous and next object in the same list. One is
///   allocated per member object and freed when the object is removed. It is
///   allocated from a pool in blocks of 256.
/// - CullSystemClass (cullsys.h) is the abstract base: the four Collect_Objects
///   overloads, Update_Culling, and the collection list the queries fill.
/// - TypedGridCullSystemClass (gridcull.h) is the template users instantiate.
///   It exposes Add_Object and Remove_Object over the protected
///   Add_Object_Internal and Remove_Object_Internal and gives typed access to
///   the collected objects.
///
/// \par Where it is used
/// Zero Hour: a search of the GeneralsMD C++ sources and headers finds one
/// user of the grid outside gridcull itself, WWAudio's SoundScene.h, which names
/// TypedGridCullSystemClass of SoundCullObjClass as DynamicSoundCullClass, the
/// culling system for moving sound objects. SoundSceneClass, declared in
/// SoundScene.h, has three data members of that type (for listeners, logical
/// sounds and dynamic sounds) next to an AABTree-based system for static sounds.
/// In SoundScene.cpp its constructor, and its own Re_Partition function, call
/// GridCullSystemClass::Re_Partition on each grid with an object size limit of
/// 100 for the dynamic and logical grids and 40 for the listener grid; the
/// constructor passes a default extent of -500 to 500 on every axis. Only the
/// dynamic and logical grids are populated and queried after that: sounds are
/// added to and removed from them, and each is queried by calling
/// Reset_Collection, then the point overload of Collect_Objects at a listener's
/// position, then walking the collected objects (the dynamic grid in
/// Collect_Audible_Sounds, the logical grid in Collect_Logical_Sounds). The
/// listener grid is only partitioned in these sources: no object is added to it
/// and it is never queried. SoundSceneClass also has an Update_Sound function
/// that passes a sound's bounding box to CullableClass::Set_Cull_Box, which is
/// how a moving sound reaches Update_Culling. The class's own Re_Partition
/// function is itself called by Save_Static_Sounds and Load_Static in Zero
/// Hour, with the scene's own stored extents.
///
/// BFME 2: a scan of the 1.06 retail executable for direct calls found none to
/// the GridCullSystemClass constructor, to Add_Object_Internal or to
/// Remove_Object_Internal, and found the class's table of virtual functions
/// referenced only from its own constructor and destructor. It also found no
/// direct call to the CullableClass constructor or to CullableClass::Set_Cull_Box.
/// On that evidence the shipped game never creates a grid culling system: the
/// code is part of the linked WWMath library but is not reached. Direct calls
/// are all the scan can see, so it does not exclude use through a mechanism
/// that does not call these addresses. Static call relationships recorded
/// elsewhere in this guide, such as the frustum overload of
/// CollisionMath::Overlap_Test being called from GridCullSystemClass, describe
/// code that is present, not code that runs in the shipped game. The scan was
/// run with tools/callers_of.py and a direct search of the executable's code
/// section; rerun them for the current picture.
///
/// \par Differences from Zero Hour
/// None in the behaviour of the functions documented here. Comparing the
/// source that reproduces them with Zero Hour's gridcull.cpp and gridcull.h,
/// ignoring comments, the code of these functions is the same.
///
/// \par Evidence used on these pages
/// - The bodies: the source that reproduces the retail code, which is Zero
///   Hour's WWMath code (the header is the one in the BFME 1 reference tree,
///   which declares the same members as Zero Hour's apart from where one
///   trivial getter is defined).
/// - The retail executable, read directly: which functions each body calls,
///   the layout of the saved chunk, the virtual-function table, and the call
///   scans above.
/// - Zero Hour sources only: how SoundSceneClass drives the grid, and the
///   semantics of the CollisionMath overlap tests the leaf scans call.
///
/// \par Modding
/// On the evidence above (a scan of the retail executable for direct calls, which
/// does not exclude use through a mechanism that does not call these
/// addresses), nothing in the shipped game is known to construct this class, so
/// no game data is known to reach it: no INI field, script action, string table
/// entry or asset is known to change its behaviour. Its tunables are constants in
/// code and values its owner passes to Re_Partition: a default minimum cell size
/// of 10 world units on every axis (changeable with Set_Min_Cell_Size, and
/// effective at the next Re_Partition), a default world of -100 to 100 on every
/// axis with an object size limit of 15, and a cap of 16384 cells
/// (Set_Termination_Count).
/// If a mod or a rebuilt executable begins to use the class from game logic,
/// treat it as an acceleration structure that filters objects by their cull
/// boxes: the order of a collection is a by-product of link history and visiting
/// order and is not stable, so logic that must stay in lockstep across peers
/// should not depend on it.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The algorithm is single-precision float arithmetic plus integer list
///   handling, with no Direct3D or audio dependency. The platform seams are the
///   pool that link records come from (see the lock note below) and the chunk
///   IO and allocator that Save, Load and the cell array use.
/// - Cell indices come from the C runtime's floor on a double and an
///   unchecked conversion to int, and the retail code does not convert the
///   three axes alike (see GridCullSystemClass::map_point_to_cell). A
///   coordinate far enough from the grid to overflow the conversion therefore
///   gives a platform-specific index. A query volume that is meant to be
///   unbounded should be clamped to the grid's extent by the caller rather than
///   given huge coordinates.
/// - The class keeps no state per thread. A query writes the collection chain
///   (a head pointer in the system and a chain pointer inside each object), so
///   even queries mutate shared state: concurrent queries over one set of
///   objects need external serialisation. An object can belong to only one
///   culling system (the source asserts, in debug builds, that an object being
///   added has none), so a separate system per thread works only with disjoint
///   sets of objects.
/// - The saved form (Save and Load) holds only floats and 32-bit counts, with no
///   pointers, so it is clean for a 64-bit build. In memory the cell array and
///   link records hold pointers, and the source sizes the cell array from the
///   pointer size rather than a fixed 4.
/// - The pool that link records come from takes a small lock in its allocation
///   path in the retail code, so creating a link is cheap but not free of
///   synchronisation.
/// - The number of cells is capped, so on a very large world the cells grow
///   with the world and hold more objects each (see
///   GridCullSystemClass::Re_Partition). A remaster with a much larger world or
///   many more objects would raise the cap or choose another structure.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass


/// \file WWMath/gridcull.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_wwmath_gridcull
/// \brief The out-of-line members of GridCullSystemClass and GridLinkClass,
///        and the Save and Load chunk format.
///
/// The queries (the four Collect_Objects overloads and the leaf scans they use),
/// the partitioning (Re_Partition and its helper that pulls every object out of
/// the grid), the linking functions and the chunk Save and Load live here. Some
/// small helpers are inline in gridcull.h, so the compiler emits their bodies
/// again in this unit. This group documents the inline map_point_to_cell and
/// map_point_to_address and the init_volume overloads listed on this page, not
/// every inline helper in that header. The file
/// declares the pool that GridLinkClass objects come from and the file-local
/// constants of the chunk format: the format version and the identifiers of the
/// version chunk and the parameters chunk.


/// \class GridCullSystemClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1024a_wwmath_gridcull
/// \brief A CullSystemClass that keeps objects in a flat array of cell lists,
///        keyed by the centre of each object's cull box, plus one overflow list
///        for objects that do not fit.
///
/// The system divides a box-shaped world into a regular lattice of cells. Each
/// cell holds a list of the objects whose cull-box centres fall inside it.
/// Objects too big for the grid, or centred outside it, live in one more list,
/// the no-grid list, which every query scans in full. A query
/// converts its volume to a range of cells, scans the lists of those cells and
/// the no-grid list, and appends every object whose cull box passes the query's
/// own test to the collection list that CullSystemClass keeps.
///
/// \par Geometry
/// - The grid has an origin (the minimum corner of the world), a cell size
///   per axis and a cell count per axis. Cell addresses run along x fastest,
///   then y, then z, so a cell's address is its x index plus its y index times
///   the x count plus its z index times the x count times the y count.
/// - Re_Partition fixes the cell counts. Each axis count is a power of two (1,
///   2, 4 and so on). The default world after construction has 16 cells on
///   each axis, 12.5 world units each, 4096 cells in all.
/// - An object's cell is the one holding the centre of its cull box
///   (map_point_to_cell). Only the centre is used to place an object; its size
///   matters just for one test: if any half-size of the cull box exceeds the
///   system's object size limit, the object goes to the no-grid list instead.
/// - Queries make up for the placement rule by inflating. Every query volume is
///   grown by the object size limit on each side before it is converted to a
///   cell range (init_volume), so any gridded object whose box reaches into the
///   query has its centre in a scanned cell. This is the reason the object
///   size limit exists, and the reason an object bigger than it cannot be
///   gridded.
/// - A cell's list is doubly linked through the members' link records, new
///   members at the head. The no-grid list is the same kind of list.
///
/// \par Invariants
/// - Every member has exactly one link record and is on exactly one list: the
///   cell list at its link's address, or the no-grid list when that address is
///   the ungridded sentinel (all bits set, read as -1 in the int field).
/// - The number of members is the value Get_Object_Count returns, which the
///   system raises when an object is added and lowers when one is removed.
/// - A gridded object's box was within the size limit when it was last linked.
///   Update_Culling re-links an object only when the cell holding its centre
///   changes, so a box that grows past the limit while its centre stays in
///   one cell is not moved to the no-grid list until it changes cell or the
///   grid is re-partitioned. Queries can miss such an object when it reaches
///   beyond the inflated range. Owners that resize objects should keep them under
///   the limit, or choose a larger limit in Re_Partition.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - The system owns the cell array, allocated as an array of pointers sized to
///   the cell count and cleared, and the link records. It does not own the
///   objects; it takes one reference on each when they are added
///   (Add_Object_Internal) and gives it back when they are removed
///   (Remove_Object_Internal).
/// - The destructor frees only the cell array. It does not unlink members,
///   release their references or free their link records, and the link records
///   hold a pointer back to the system. Remove every object before destroying
///   a grid. The destructor frees the array with a plain delete, where the code
///   that allocates it uses array new; for an array of raw pointers on this
///   compiler the two are equivalent, and a port to another compiler should
///   use array delete.
/// - A grid is created empty and already partitioned: the constructor calls
///   Re_Partition for a default world. Owners then call Re_Partition with the real
///   extent before adding objects, or at any time after, since Re_Partition
///   re-files all members.
///
/// \par The collection list
/// The query functions do not return a list. They append matches to the
/// collection that CullSystemClass keeps (a head pointer in the system and a
/// chain pointer inside each object), and the owner then walks it with the
/// typed iteration functions of TypedGridCullSystemClass. This has consequences
/// that follow from the code:
/// - A query adds to the list it finds. Call CullSystemClass::Reset_Collection
///   first to start a new list; Westwood's header says to always do so.
/// - Because the chain pointer lives inside the object, an object can be on a
///   collection once. Running two queries into one list without a reset can
///   add an object twice, which links it to itself or into a loop and makes
///   the walk endless.
/// - The collection holds no references. Removing an object that is also on a
///   collection built earlier leaves a stale entry, and releasing the last
///   reference frees the object under it.
/// - New entries are pushed at the head, so the walk visits objects in the
///   reverse of the order they were found. Within a query the order found is:
///   the cells in ascending address, each cell's list from its head, then the
///   no-grid list. Treat the order as unspecified.
/// - Re_Partition and Load reset the collection themselves, and use it to hold
///   the members while the grid is rebuilt, so they destroy a collection
///   the caller built.
///
/// \par Statistics
/// The system has a block of counters for visited and accepted nodes, which the
/// query functions bump through macros in gridcull.h. The macros expand to
/// nothing unless debug checking is compiled in, and the retail code contains no
/// such updates. Reset_Statistics sets the virtual node count (twice the cell
/// count, less one) and clears the three counters; the constructor calls it.
/// Re_Partition does not call it again, so after a re-partition the node count
/// still describes the constructor's default grid until Reset_Statistics is
/// called. The three counters stay zero because nothing in the retail code
/// updates them.
///
/// \par Reading the retail binary
/// The small protected helpers are inline in the header, and in the retail code
/// most of them are expanded in their callers, so a call from Collect_Objects
/// to init_volume, map_point_to_cell or clamp_indices_to_grid will not appear in a
/// disassembly. Stand-alone copies of those helpers exist as well, with no direct
/// caller. Likewise the leaf scans for the oriented-box and frustum queries are
/// expanded inside the cell loop of their Collect_Objects overloads; only the
/// scan of the no-grid list is an out-of-line call. The four Collect_Objects
/// overloads sit in the virtual-function table in the reverse of their
/// declaration order. Do not assume declaration order when calling or patching
/// through the table.
///
/// \par Persistence
/// Save and Load write and read the grid's partition, not its members. The
/// members are whatever objects are in the system when Load runs; they are
/// re-filed into the loaded partition. See GridCullSystemClass::Save and
/// GridCullSystemClass::Load for the chunk layout.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The class, its members and the algorithms are Westwood's (Zero Hour's
/// WWMath code). Behaviour on this page is read from the matching bodies.
/// Where the retail executable was read directly, the page says so. The
/// Zero Hour usage notes are from Zero Hour sources and are not BFME 2 facts.
///
/// \par Modding and remastering
/// See the notes on the group page, \ref grp_w1024a_wwmath_gridcull.
///
/// \see CullSystemClass, CullableClass, AABoxClass, OBBoxClass, FrustumClass,
///      CollisionMath


/// \fn GridCullSystemClass::GridCullSystemClass()
/// \brief Builds an empty, already partitioned grid culling system with a
///        default 200-unit world.
///
/// Runs the CullSystemClass constructor (an empty collection), sets the default
/// parameters and then calls GridCullSystemClass::Re_Partition once, so the cell
/// array always exists afterwards. Owners are expected to call Re_Partition
/// again with their real world extent before adding objects. Zero Hour's
/// SoundSceneClass calls it on each of its grids from its own constructor, with a
/// default extent of -500 to 500 on every axis.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Minimum cell size 10 world units on every axis, object size limit 15,
///   cell-count cap 16384, no cells yet, empty no-grid list, no members.
/// - The initial world is -100 to 100 on every axis. The partition loop of
///   Re_Partition turns that into 16 cells on each axis, each 12.5 units, for
///   4096 cells, because a 12.5-unit cell cannot be halved without going below
///   the minimum cell size.
/// - The call to Re_Partition is bound to this class's own version, as any
///   virtual call from a constructor is, so a derived class cannot influence the
///   initial partition.
/// - Last, it calls Reset_Statistics, which sets the virtual node count (twice
///   the cell count, less one) and clears the three counters.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The default values (the cell sizes, object size limit, world corners and
/// cell cap) and the call order were checked in the retail constructor. The retail constructor also stores the class's table of
/// virtual functions, which only the constructor and destructor reference.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Re_Partition


/// \fn virtual void GridCullSystemClass::Re_Partition(const Vector3 &min, const Vector3 &max, float objdim)
/// \brief Rebuilds the grid for a new world box and a new object size limit,
///        and files every member again.
///
/// This is how an owner tells the system how big the world is. It can be called
/// at any time: members are taken out, the cell array is rebuilt and they are
/// put back, so no object is lost whatever the new parameters. The constructor
/// calls it with a default world. Zero Hour's SoundSceneClass partitions each of
/// its grids in its constructor over a default extent, and again from its own
/// Re_Partition function, which it also calls around saving and loading static
/// sounds.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// 1. Reset the collection, then pull every member out of the cells and the
///    no-grid list onto the collection (the helper Collect_And_Unlink_All).
/// 2. Sanity-check the box. An axis whose extent is under one world unit
///    (including one whose corners are inverted) is widened by the minimum cell
///    size on each side, so a flat world still gets a cell of usable thickness.
///    The corrected minimum corner becomes the grid origin and the object size
///    limit becomes objdim.
/// 3. Choose the cell counts. Start with one cell spanning the whole box. Then
///    repeat: find the axis whose cell size is largest relative to the minimum
///    cell size on that axis (ties go to the lower axis); if that cell size is at
///    least twice the minimum, halve it and double that axis's cell count.
///    Stop after a pass in which the total cell count has reached the cap, or
///    in which no axis can be halved any more.
/// 4. Store the reciprocal cell sizes that the cell lookup multiplies by.
/// 5. Free the old cell array, allocate a new one sized to the cell count, and
///    clear it.
/// 6. Link every object on the collection back in, which places each by its
///    cull-box centre and may send it to the no-grid list under the new limit.
///
/// Consequences of step 3: an axis is never split below the minimum cell size
/// (an axis whose world extent is already smaller stays one cell); every axis
/// count is a power of two; because the axis with the largest cell size relative
/// to the minimum is always halved next, cell shapes stay balanced in those
/// units, though the cap can stop the loop with elongated cells; and the cap
/// acts as a threshold rather than a bound. The loop stops after the split that
/// reaches or passes it, and as the total is a power of two, a cap that is not a
/// power of two is overshot to the next power of two.
///
/// \param min    Minimum corner of the world, in the same space as the members'
///               cull boxes.
/// \param max    Maximum corner of the world. An axis whose extent is under one
///               unit is widened as described above. An axis inverted by more
///               than twice the minimum cell size is still inverted after that
///               and is not rejected.
/// \param objdim Object size limit: the largest half-size on any axis that an
///               object may have and still be kept in a cell. It is also the
///               amount queries inflate their volumes by. Larger objects go to
///               the no-grid list.
///
/// \note The collection is destroyed: the caller's pending collection is lost.
///       Call this between queries, not while holding a collection.
/// \note The checks that the box is non-degenerate are debug-build assertions
///       and do not exist in the retail code. Do not pass NaN or inverted
///       corners.
/// \note The cost is proportional to the member count plus the cell count, and
///       it allocates. Avoid calling it per frame.
///
/// \par Tunables
/// The partition depends on the minimum cell size and the cell cap. The header
/// gives both setters (Set_Min_Cell_Size and Set_Termination_Count), and a new
/// value takes effect only at the next call to this function.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::GridCullSystemClass, GridCullSystemClass::Load,
///      GridCullSystemClass::link_object


/// \fn virtual void GridCullSystemClass::Update_Culling(CullableClass *obj)
/// \brief Re-files a member whose cull box has changed, if its centre moved to
///        a different cell.
///
/// CullableClass::Set_Cull_Box calls this on the object's owning system after
/// storing a new box (unless told the object was just loaded), so the call is how
/// a moving object keeps its place in the grid. It is cheap when the object
/// stays in its cell: one cell lookup and one compare.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// It looks up the address of the cell holding the centre of the object's cull
/// box and compares it with the address in the object's link. If they are equal
/// nothing else happens. Otherwise it unlinks the object from its current list
/// and links it to the new address using link_object with an address, which
/// re-applies the size limit and the in-grid test.
///
/// A consequence worth knowing: the size limit is tested only when the object
/// is linked. An object that is in the no-grid list because it is too big, but
/// whose centre is inside the grid, has the sentinel in its link while the newly
/// computed address is a real cell. The two never match, so each call moves it
/// out of the no-grid list and straight back in. That is harmless but wasteful.
/// In the other direction, an object that grows past the limit without leaving
/// its cell stays in the cell (see the invariants on the class page).
///
/// \param obj A member of this system. It must have been added with
///            Add_Object_Internal; using any other object dereferences its
///            missing link record.
///
/// \note The debug-only assertions that the object belongs to this system do not
///       exist in the retail code.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::link_object, GridCullSystemClass::unlink_object,
///      GridCullSystemClass::map_point_to_address


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::Remove_Object_Internal(CullableClass *obj)
/// \brief Takes an object out of the system, frees its link record and gives
///        back the reference the system held.
///
/// This is the protected half of the Remove_Object function of
/// TypedGridCullSystemClass. It is the only way an object leaves the system, and
/// the grid does nothing for a member that is still present when the system is
/// destroyed.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The order matters:
/// 1. Unlink the object from the list it is on.
/// 2. Clear the link's pointer to the system. In the source, the link record's
///    destructor asserts in debug builds that this pointer is already clear,
///    which is why it is cleared first.
/// 3. Free the link record and clear the object's link pointer, which is what
///    makes the object report that it is in no culling system.
/// 4. Lower the member count and release the reference taken at add time. If that
///    was the last reference, the object is destroyed here.
///
/// \param obj A member of this system.
///
/// \warning Releasing the reference can destroy the object. An object that also
///          sits on a collection built earlier, which holds no reference of its
///          own, is left dangling there. Reset or rebuild the collection first.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::unlink_object, GridCullSystemClass::Update_Culling


/// \fn virtual void GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const Vector3 &point)
/// \brief Adds to the collection every member whose cull box contains a point.
///
/// This is the query Zero Hour's SoundSceneClass uses on its dynamic and logical
/// grids, at a listener's position, to find the sounds around that listener.
/// The other three overloads share this function's structure and differ in the
/// volume they turn into a cell range and in the per-object test.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// 1. Turn the point into a range of cells with init_volume, using the point as
///    both corners of the box. Because init_volume inflates by the object size
///    limit, the range covers every cell that could hold an object whose box
///    contains the point.
/// 2. If the range is not empty, visit the cells in it, x fastest, and for each
///    call collect_objects_in_leaf on the cell's list. The range is clamped to
///    the grid, so a point outside the world still scans the nearest edge cells.
/// 3. Always scan the no-grid list as well, with the same test.
///
/// The per-object test is that the object's cull box contains the point, with the
/// surface counting as inside. Matches are pushed onto the collection. The query
/// does not clear the collection first.
///
/// \param point The query position, in the same space as the members' cull
///              boxes.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// In the retail code the point query calls the out-of-line leaf scan once
/// per cell and once for the no-grid list, with the range set-up and cell
/// lookup expanded inline. No statistics are updated.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const AABoxClass &),
///      GridCullSystemClass::collect_objects_in_leaf,
///      GridCullSystemClass::init_volume


/// \fn virtual void GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const AABoxClass &box)
/// \brief Adds to the collection every member whose cull box overlaps an
///        axis-aligned box.
///
/// Same traversal as the point query. The box is converted to a cell range by
/// init_volume (its minimum and maximum corners, grown by the object size
/// limit), the cells in the range and then the no-grid list are scanned, and an
/// object is collected unless CollisionMath::Overlap_Test, with the query box as
/// the first operand and the object's cull box as the second, says it is
/// outside.
///
/// Objects that touch the query box, overlap it, or lie wholly inside it are all
/// collected; a query box that lies inside an object's box is collected too, as
/// an overlap. Two boxes that exactly touch count as overlapping. The result is
/// a pure box-against-box test on cull boxes, so it is only as tight as those
/// boxes are.
///
/// \param box The query volume, as a centre and half-sizes, in the same space
///            as the members' cull boxes.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// Behaviour is read from the body and from Zero Hour's box-against-box overlap
/// test, which the leaf scan uses. In the retail code this overload calls the
/// out-of-line leaf scan once per cell and once for the no-grid list.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const Vector3 &),
///      GridCullSystemClass::collect_objects_in_leaf,
///      CollisionMath::Overlap_Test


/// \fn virtual void GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const OBBoxClass &box)
/// \brief Adds to the collection every member whose cull box overlaps an
///        oriented box.
///
/// The oriented box is reduced to its enclosing axis-aligned box to pick the cell
/// range (init_volume, which asks the box for the half-sizes of the axis-aligned
/// box around it), so the grid narrows the candidates to the cells that bound
/// the box; the per-object test then checks the oriented shape. The test is
/// CollisionMath::Overlap_Test with the oriented box as the first operand and
/// the object's cull box, an axis-aligned box, as the second. In Zero Hour's
/// source that overload answers either outside or overlapping, never inside,
/// from an oriented-box against axis-aligned-box intersection test, so every
/// object that is not outside is collected.
///
/// A box that is rotated 45 degrees has an enclosing box much larger than
/// itself, so more cells are scanned than the box's own volume would need.
///
/// \param box The query volume, with its own orientation, in the same space as
///            the members' cull boxes.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// In the retail code the oriented box's enclosing half-size is computed by an
/// out-of-line call, and the leaf scan for the cells is expanded inside the cell
/// loop (calling the overlap test and the collection-add function directly).
/// Only the no-grid list goes through the out-of-line leaf scan. The semantics
/// of the overlap test come from Zero Hour sources.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const AABoxClass &),
///      GridCullSystemClass::init_volume(const OBBoxClass &, VolumeStruct *)


/// \fn virtual void GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const FrustumClass &frustum)
/// \brief Adds to the collection every member whose cull box is not wholly
///        outside a view frustum.
///
/// The visibility query. The frustum's own axis-aligned bound picks the cell
/// range (init_volume); the cells in it and the no-grid list are scanned; an
/// object is collected unless CollisionMath::Overlap_Test for a frustum says its
/// cull box is outside. The frustum overload is conservative: it answers outside
/// only when the box is wholly in front of one of the six planes, so objects near
/// a frustum edge or corner can be collected without being visible. Inside and
/// overlapping both collect the object. The caller still sees objects that
/// the later render stages cull more precisely.
///
/// A frustum that is turned relative to the axes has an axis-aligned bound much
/// bigger than itself. A wide camera far plane over a large map therefore makes
/// this query visit many cells, each of whose objects goes through the plane test.
/// The inflated range is the cost of the grid being uniform.
///
/// \param frustum The view volume, with outward-facing plane normals, in the same
///                space as the members' cull boxes.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// In the retail code the leaf scan for the cells is expanded inside the cell
/// loop; only the no-grid list goes through the out-of-line leaf scan. See the
/// page for CollisionMath::Overlap_Test for the frustum and axis-aligned-box
/// overload used here.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const AABoxClass &),
///      GridCullSystemClass::init_volume(const FrustumClass &, VolumeStruct *),
///      CollisionMath::Overlap_Test


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::collect_objects_in_leaf(const Vector3 &point, CullableClass *head)
/// \brief Scans one list of members (a cell, or the no-grid list) and collects
///        those whose cull box contains a point.
///
/// Helper of the point overload of Collect_Objects. It walks the list from
/// head with a GridListIterator, which follows the next links in the members'
/// link records, and pushes each match onto the collection. The test is the
/// containment test of the object's cull box, which counts the surface as inside.
/// No reference is taken on the objects collected.
///
/// \param point The query position.
/// \param head  First object of the list to scan; null for an empty list, which
///              does nothing.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const Vector3 &)


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::collect_objects_in_leaf(const AABoxClass &aabox, CullableClass *head)
/// \brief Scans one list of members and collects those whose cull box overlaps
///        an axis-aligned box.
///
/// Same walk as the point version. An object is collected unless the overlap
/// test, with the query box first and the object's cull box second, answers
/// outside. Inside and overlapping both collect.
///
/// \param aabox The query box.
/// \param head  First object of the list to scan; null for an empty list.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const AABoxClass &)


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::collect_objects_in_leaf(const OBBoxClass &obbox, CullableClass *head)
/// \brief Scans one list of members and collects those whose cull box overlaps
///        an oriented box.
///
/// Same walk as the point version, with the oriented-box overlap test (query box
/// first, object's axis-aligned cull box second) and the outside answer
/// rejecting. In the retail code this function is called for the no-grid list
/// only; the cell lists are scanned by the same logic expanded inside
/// Collect_Objects.
///
/// \param obbox The query box, with its orientation.
/// \param head  First object of the list to scan; null for an empty list.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const OBBoxClass &)


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::collect_objects_in_leaf(const FrustumClass &frustum, CullableClass *head)
/// \brief Scans one list of members and collects those whose cull box is not
///        wholly outside a frustum.
///
/// Same walk as the point version, with the frustum overlap test against each
/// object's cull box. Because that test is conservative, every object it does not
/// reject is collected, including some that are outside the frustum near its
/// edges. In the retail code this function is called for the no-grid list only;
/// the cell lists are scanned by the same logic expanded inside Collect_Objects.
///
/// \param frustum The view volume.
/// \param head    First object of the list to scan; null for an empty list.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const FrustumClass &)


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::init_volume(const Vector3 &bound_min, const Vector3 &bound_max, VolumeStruct *set_volume)
/// \brief Converts a world-space box into the range of grid cells that a query
///        must scan.
///
/// The AABoxClass, OBBoxClass and FrustumClass overloads documented below all
/// reduce to this one, as does the line-segment overload in the header. The
/// header also declares a point overload, which it does not define. The range is
/// half open:
/// the first index on each axis is the first cell to visit and the second index is
/// one past the last. Because queries must also find the large objects that
/// protrude from neighbouring cells, the box is grown by the object size limit
/// on every side before it is converted.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// 1. Subtract the object size limit from the minimum corner and add it to the
///    maximum corner on every axis.
/// 2. Find the cell of each corner with map_point_to_cell. The result of that
///    call is ignored: it writes the indices even when the point is outside the
///    grid, and the next step repairs them.
/// 3. Clamp both index triples into the grid.
/// 4. Add one to each maximum index so the range is half open.
///
/// Consequences:
/// - A box entirely beyond one face of the world clamps to the outermost layer
///   of cells, so it still scans that layer. A box is never turned into zero cells
///   by lying outside the world.
/// - The range is empty only for an inverted box, whose maximum corner is below
///   the minimum corner by more than the inflation covers. A valid query never
///   produces one, because clamping always leaves at least one cell. Callers
///   test for it and skip the grid in that case, scanning only the no-grid list.
/// - There is no overflow check on the conversion to cell indices; see the
///   remastering notes on GridCullSystemClass::map_point_to_cell.
///
/// \param bound_min  Minimum corner of the world-space box.
/// \param bound_max  Maximum corner of the world-space box.
/// \param set_volume [out] The cell range: first and last-plus-one index on each
///                   axis.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::map_point_to_cell
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Collect_Objects(const Vector3 &)


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::init_volume(const AABoxClass &box, VolumeStruct *set_volume)
/// \brief Cell range for an axis-aligned box.
///
/// Passes the box's centre minus its half-size and centre plus its half-size to
/// the two-corner version, which does the work and grows the box by the object
/// size limit.
///
/// \param box        The query box.
/// \param set_volume [out] The cell range.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::init_volume(const Vector3 &, const Vector3 &, VolumeStruct *)


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::init_volume(const OBBoxClass &box, VolumeStruct *set_volume)
/// \brief Cell range for an oriented box, taken from its enclosing
///        axis-aligned box.
///
/// Asks the oriented box for the half-sizes of the axis-aligned box around it,
/// which it computes as the projection of the rotated half-sizes onto each axis.
/// It then passes the box's centre minus and plus those half-sizes to the
/// two-corner version. The range therefore covers the whole enclosing box, even
/// where the rotated box does not reach; the per-object test rejects the extra
/// candidates.
///
/// \param box        The oriented query box.
/// \param set_volume [out] The cell range.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::init_volume(const Vector3 &, const Vector3 &, VolumeStruct *)


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::init_volume(const FrustumClass &frustum, VolumeStruct *set_volume)
/// \brief Cell range for a view frustum, taken from the frustum's own
///        axis-aligned bound.
///
/// Passes the frustum's precomputed minimum and maximum bound, which
/// FrustumClass::Init builds from the frustum's eight corners, to the
/// two-corner version. The range covers the whole bound, not just the frustum
/// inside it.
///
/// \param frustum    The view frustum.
/// \param set_volume [out] The cell range.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::init_volume(const Vector3 &, const Vector3 &, VolumeStruct *),
///      FrustumClass::Init


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::link_object(CullableClass *obj)
/// \brief Files an object under the cell that holds the centre of its cull box.
///
/// Used when an object is added and when the grid is rebuilt. It looks up the
/// cell address of the centre of the object's cull box and hands it to the
/// version that takes an address. An object whose centre lies outside the grid
/// gets the ungridded sentinel address, which sends it to the no-grid list.
///
/// \param obj A member that already has a link record. It is not on any list
///            yet.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::link_object(CullableClass *, int),
///      GridCullSystemClass::map_point_to_address


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::link_object(CullableClass *obj, int address)
/// \brief Puts an object on the list for a given cell, or on the no-grid list
///        if it does not fit.
///
/// The single place that decides which list an object belongs on. It uses the
/// no-grid list when any half-size of the object's cull box is larger than the
/// object size limit, or when the address is the ungridded sentinel. Otherwise
/// it files the object in the cell at that address. Either way it records the
/// address (or the sentinel) in the object's link, so unlink_object can find the
/// list again, and pushes the object at the head of the list.
///
/// \param obj     A member with a link record, off every list.
/// \param address Cell address from map_point_to_address, or the ungridded
///                sentinel. Not range-checked: an address outside the cell
///                array writes outside it.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::link_object_to_list,
///      GridCullSystemClass::Update_Culling


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::unlink_object(CullableClass *obj)
/// \brief Takes an object off the list it is on.
///
/// Reads the address stored in the object's link and removes the object from the
/// no-grid list if the address is the ungridded sentinel, otherwise from that
/// cell's list. The link record stays attached to the object. The caller decides
/// whether to link it again (Update_Culling, Re_Partition) or free the record
/// (Remove_Object_Internal).
///
/// \param obj A member that is on one of the system's lists.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::unlink_object_from_list,
///      GridCullSystemClass::Remove_Object_Internal


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::link_object_to_list(CullableClass **head, CullableClass *obj)
/// \brief Pushes an object at the head of a doubly linked list of members.
///
/// The lists are intrusive: the next and previous pointers live in each
/// object's link record, and the head is a plain pointer, either a cell in the
/// array or the no-grid list's head. Insertion sets the object's next to the old
/// head, clears its previous, points the old head's previous back at the object
/// and makes the object the head. It takes constant time.
///
/// \param head [in,out] The list's head pointer.
/// \param obj  The object to add. It must not already be on a list.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::unlink_object_from_list


/// \fn void GridCullSystemClass::unlink_object_from_list(CullableClass **head, CullableClass *obj)
/// \brief Removes an object from a doubly linked list of members in constant
///        time.
///
/// If the object is the head, the head moves to the next object. The neighbours
/// are joined to each other through their link records, and the object's own
/// previous and next pointers are cleared. There is no search: the list was not
/// scanned to find the object.
///
/// \param head [in,out] The list's head pointer.
/// \param obj  An object that is on that list. Removing an object that is not
///             on the list corrupts it.
///
/// \note The scan that checks the object is really on the list exists only in
///       debug builds. The retail code has no such check, so passing the wrong
///       list head damages that list silently.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::link_object_to_list


/// \fn bool GridCullSystemClass::map_point_to_address(const Vector3 &pt, int &set_address)
/// \brief Finds the address of the cell that holds a point, or reports that the
///        point is outside the grid.
///
/// Combines map_point_to_cell with the address formula: x index plus the x
/// count times (y index plus the y count times z index). When the cell lookup
/// fails it stores the ungridded sentinel, all bits set, in the output.
///
/// \param pt          The position to locate.
/// \param set_address [out] The cell address, or the ungridded sentinel when the
///                    point is outside the grid.
/// \return true if the point is inside the grid, false otherwise.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::map_point_to_cell, GridCullSystemClass::link_object


/// \fn bool GridCullSystemClass::map_point_to_cell(const Vector3 &pt, int &set_i, int &set_j, int &set_k)
/// \brief Finds the cell indices of a point and tells whether they lie inside
///        the grid.
///
/// Subtracts the origin, multiplies each component by the reciprocal cell size
/// and floors it, so a point on a cell boundary belongs to the cell above it.
/// The indices are written even when the point is outside the grid; the return
/// value says whether all three are in range (not negative and below the cell
/// count of their axis). init_volume depends on this: it ignores the result and
/// clamps the indices it gets back.
///
/// \param pt    The position to locate.
/// \param set_i [out] Cell index on the x axis.
/// \param set_j [out] Cell index on the y axis.
/// \param set_k [out] Cell index on the z axis.
/// \return true if the point is inside the grid, false if any index is out of
///         range.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The retail code does this arithmetic with SSE for the subtract and multiply
/// and with x87 for the rest: it calls the C runtime's floor on a double for
/// each axis and converts to int with the x87 truncating store (the third axis
/// through the C runtime's float-to-int helper). That is a property of the
/// compiler used, not something the algorithm requires.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// There is no range check before the conversion, and the retail code does not
/// treat the axes alike. The first two use a 32-bit x87 store, which turns an
/// out-of-range value into the most negative int (so a point far beyond the high
/// side reads as outside on the low side). The third goes through the C runtime's
/// float-to-int helper, whose result for an out-of-range value is specific to
/// that helper. A saturating conversion on another platform would behave
/// differently again. Keep coordinates handed to the grid inside the range of
/// an int measured in cells from the origin, and clamp query volumes that are
/// meant to be unbounded.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::map_point_to_address,
///      GridCullSystemClass::init_volume


/// \fn virtual void GridCullSystemClass::Save(ChunkSaveClass &csave)
/// \brief Writes the grid's partition (not its members) to a chunk stream.
///
/// Writes two chunks, in order. The first, with identifier 1, holds one 32-bit
/// version number, the format version constant of the source (high 16 bits 1, low
/// 16 bits 0). The second, with identifier 0x100, holds the grid parameters as 13
/// four-byte fields with no padding, in this order: the minimum cell size (three
/// floats), the origin (three floats), the cell size (three floats), the cell
/// count on each axis (three 32-bit unsigned integers) and the object size limit
/// (one float). The members are not written, and neither are the reciprocal cell
/// size, the cell-count cap or the statistics.
///
/// \param csave The chunk writer. It must be positioned where a chunk may begin.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The version value, both chunk identifiers and the parameters chunk size were
/// read in the retail function.
///
/// \par Modding
/// No code in the retail executable calls this function directly; it is referenced
/// only from the class's table of virtual functions. A tool that reads or writes
/// the chunk needs only the layout above.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Load


/// \fn virtual void GridCullSystemClass::Load(ChunkLoadClass &cload)
/// \brief Reads a partition written by Save and re-files the current members
///        into it.
///
/// Reads the version chunk and the parameters chunk (see
/// GridCullSystemClass::Save for the layout), then rebuilds the grid from the
/// stored numbers instead of working out the cell counts again. The members
/// are whatever objects are in the system now: the function pulls them all
/// out (the helper Collect_And_Unlink_All, which also resets the collection),
/// replaces the grid parameters with the loaded ones, recomputes the reciprocal
/// cell size, frees and reallocates the cell array for the loaded cell counts,
/// and links each member back in by its current cull box. Objects now outside the
/// loaded world, or larger than the loaded object size limit, go to the no-grid
/// list.
///
/// \param cload The chunk reader, positioned at the version chunk.
///
/// \warning The retail function trusts its input. It does not compare the version
///          value or the chunk identifiers (the checks are debug-build
///          assertions), does not look at the result of opening or reading a
///          chunk, and sizes the cell array from the product of the three loaded
///          counts without checking them. A damaged or hostile chunk can request
///          an arbitrary allocation. It leaves the cell-count cap and the
///          statistics untouched.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The absence of checks was read in the retail function; the member order is
/// from the matching body.
///
/// \see GridCullSystemClass::Save, GridCullSystemClass::Re_Partition
